00:03:56.784 --> 00:04:00.011
So, writing in the holistic
review process.

00:04:00.011 --> 00:04:02.946
This is going to be a definition
that you've seen through the week.

00:04:02.946 --> 00:04:07.916
Holistic, characterized by comprehension
of the parts of something as intimately

00:04:07.916 --> 00:04:11.704
interconnected and
explicable only by reference to the whole.

00:04:11.704 --> 00:04:16.193
What that means in the admissions process
at Stanford is that we are taking a look

00:04:16.193 --> 00:04:20.281
at everything you submit for us to
really get to know and understand you.

00:04:20.281 --> 00:04:24.112
It's not a process where we're looking at
one thing kind of screening from there,

00:04:24.112 --> 00:04:26.424
looking at another thing and
passing it through.

00:04:26.424 --> 00:04:30.329
But really taking all the pieces
to really understand, okay,

00:04:30.329 --> 00:04:35.046
how do these elements of context come
together for us to understand, okay,

00:04:35.046 --> 00:04:40.208
here is how this individual's intellectual
vitality, demonstrated leadership

00:04:40.208 --> 00:04:44.728
potential, and personal qualities and
contributions come together.

00:04:44.728 --> 00:04:48.962
In our evaluation process, it is Is
not a formula, It's not an equation or

00:04:48.962 --> 00:04:49.712
algorithm.

00:04:49.712 --> 00:04:54.229
There are no benchmarks, cut offs or
quotas, so we're not saying, hey, we need

00:04:54.229 --> 00:04:58.689
x amount from this industry or we're only
going to take x amount from that country.

00:04:58.689 --> 00:05:00.520
That's not how it works.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:05.198
We're really looking at you as
an individual, trying to bring together

00:05:05.198 --> 00:05:10.659
a talented, dynamic, exciting group of
people to learn and kind of grow together.

00:05:10.659 --> 00:05:15.509
No one part of the application
necessarily means more than another.

00:05:15.509 --> 00:05:20.739
And I think this is where sometimes
that essay starts to look really,

00:05:20.739 --> 00:05:24.898
darkly over your anxiety,
over what can happen next.

00:05:24.898 --> 00:05:26.948
It is not a make it or break it piece.

00:05:26.948 --> 00:05:31.670
We are using the essays in conjunction
with the other parts of your application

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:33.326
to understand who you are.

00:05:33.326 --> 00:05:37.373
We are not grading your written
portions as good or bad.

00:05:37.373 --> 00:05:41.972
We're really using those as
an opportunity to learn more about you.

00:05:41.972 --> 00:05:46.673
That's one of those things that I
really care about, learning about you.

00:05:46.673 --> 00:05:50.572
So when you're out there kind of doing
research, hearing things, talking to

00:05:50.572 --> 00:05:54.249
people, and you hear someone say, hey,
this is the essay that got me in.

00:05:54.249 --> 00:05:57.198
If I take that step back and
realize, okay,

00:05:57.198 --> 00:06:01.206
that's an essay as part of
an application that got them in.

00:06:01.206 --> 00:06:04.728
I've been in this line of work long
enough, but I do remember applications.

00:06:04.728 --> 00:06:09.211
I do remember people and sometimes I hear
people say that I see them kind of in

00:06:09.211 --> 00:06:12.699
newspapers or publications and
I remember those files and

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:16.633
sometimes it's like, all right,
that's a cute thought.

00:06:16.633 --> 00:06:20.420
So some people get in
despite their essays, and

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:25.534
some people are unfortunately,
we were unable to admit despite

00:06:25.534 --> 00:06:31.138
a really well thought out introspective,
really thought out essay.

00:06:31.138 --> 00:06:36.019
So really take it as a whole,
really blowing up those parts.

00:06:36.019 --> 00:06:41.049
Admissions recommendations are made on the
evaluation as a application as a whole.

00:06:41.049 --> 00:06:46.538
If I didn't have written enough,
let's highlight that last part as a whole.

00:06:46.538 --> 00:06:50.442
Okay, intellectual vitality,
demonstrated leadership potential,

00:06:50.442 --> 00:06:52.619
personal qualities &amp; contributions.

00:06:52.619 --> 00:06:56.143
These are the things that we are looking
for throughout your application.

00:06:56.143 --> 00:06:58.185
So if you haven't done so already,

00:06:58.185 --> 00:07:02.276
check out the evaluation criteria
section of our admissions webpage.

00:07:02.276 --> 00:07:06.097
There's a lot of really great
language to help unpack and

00:07:06.097 --> 00:07:11.114
really give you new perspectives for
how to understand these somewhat kind

00:07:11.114 --> 00:07:15.912
of mushy like phrases and really see
how they might come to life for you.

00:07:15.912 --> 00:07:17.321
So in our application,

00:07:17.321 --> 00:07:21.900
that's where we were looking all of
these different pieces come together for

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:26.700
us to get that picture of who you are, how
you're going to show up in that on campus.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:31.770
When it comes to writing, you're really
going to be focusing on these four parts,

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:36.480
essays, resume, activities,
work history, and optional responses.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.172
Notice that I highlighted that section did
a little bit of kind of shading there to

00:07:41.172 --> 00:07:43.249
really highlight it's optional.

00:07:43.249 --> 00:07:45.933
We have admitted people who
have not filled those out.

00:07:45.933 --> 00:07:47.931
We have admitted people
who have filled them out.

00:07:47.931 --> 00:07:53.366
It really all depends on your journey,
the things that you're trying to share and

00:07:53.366 --> 00:07:56.777
the information that you
want us to know about you.

00:07:56.777 --> 00:08:00.989
So, I'm going to hit pause there for
a second.

00:08:00.989 --> 00:08:05.276
If you feel like you have a pretty
good understanding of how

00:08:05.276 --> 00:08:10.263
the holistic review process works,
can you jump into the Q&amp;A box,

00:08:10.263 --> 00:08:14.577
and type in the name of any animal,
just throw it in there.

00:08:14.577 --> 00:08:17.769
I want to make sure that hopefully,
people are getting a sense for

00:08:17.769 --> 00:08:19.316
the theme that's happening.

00:08:19.316 --> 00:08:23.013
Asama Cz was Canada's elephants,
there's actually no reason

00:08:23.013 --> 00:08:26.445
that I chose animals other than
to keep you all on your toes.

00:08:26.445 --> 00:08:31.008
If you do have questions about
the holistic review process fix those to

00:08:31.008 --> 00:08:33.807
please put them into that Q&amp;A box as well.

00:08:33.807 --> 00:08:38.388
I'll go through the questions afterwards
to see, hey, where are other places that I

00:08:38.388 --> 00:08:41.695
can work on to make sure we're
able to convey the knowledge and

00:08:41.695 --> 00:08:43.301
information that we need to.

00:08:43.301 --> 00:08:48.100
So any information, questions, animals,
I love animals, pop them in there.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:53.279
So, thank you for, [LAUGH] letting
us take that quick timeout.

00:08:53.279 --> 00:08:56.145
Now, let's talk writing.

00:08:56.145 --> 00:08:59.274
So content like what is it
I should be writing about?

00:08:59.274 --> 00:09:01.750
Remember that big picture of who you are?

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:06.514
How do we start to figure out which
details are going to be the most helpful?

00:09:06.514 --> 00:09:10.928
So, I often get asked questions and
they fall into a couple of buckets here.

00:09:10.928 --> 00:09:13.864
Should I be talking about personal things?

00:09:13.864 --> 00:09:15.938
Should I be talking about
professional things?

00:09:15.938 --> 00:09:17.796
What's the right balance?

00:09:17.796 --> 00:09:21.493
Should I focus on details or
a bigger picture?

00:09:21.493 --> 00:09:28.755
The answer I give is surprisingly common
across all of those and it's up to you.

00:09:28.755 --> 00:09:34.982
Why this is such an open-ended answer,
is that everybody has their own journey.

00:09:34.982 --> 00:09:40.649
For one person, your response might be
informed more by your personal experience.

00:09:40.649 --> 00:09:45.063
For someone else that balance might
swing more towards that other direction.

00:09:45.063 --> 00:09:48.888
And for me to prescribe some
kind of percentage or balance or

00:09:48.888 --> 00:09:51.947
way that you should be
kind of answering these,

00:09:51.947 --> 00:09:56.552
I think takes away some of that agency and
really does you a disservice.

00:09:56.552 --> 00:09:59.634
But that doesn't mean
we're leaving you hanging.

00:09:59.634 --> 00:10:05.876
So, take the questions as you're guide,
think of them as your friends.

00:10:05.876 --> 00:10:10.279
We put in a lot of efforts to make sure
that the questions are asking what we're

00:10:10.279 --> 00:10:11.105
looking for.

00:10:11.105 --> 00:10:17.448
And when really not trying to ask for any
hidden questions it's not a trick at all.

00:10:17.448 --> 00:10:22.917
But really, how are we learning about
you through the lens of this question?

00:10:22.917 --> 00:10:25.299
So, let's go through that in
a little bit more detail.

00:10:25.299 --> 00:10:28.135
Let's give it some little
meat on those bones.

00:10:28.135 --> 00:10:30.684
So what are these sections asking?

00:10:30.684 --> 00:10:34.426
Here are those kind of major written
portions of the GSB application.

00:10:34.426 --> 00:10:38.757
Let's start with the resume and
the activities and work history.

00:10:38.757 --> 00:10:42.474
Here we're really trying to understand,
what have you done personally,

00:10:42.474 --> 00:10:44.309
what have you done professionally?

00:10:44.309 --> 00:10:48.959
And I like to think how
these two work together.

00:10:48.959 --> 00:10:52.794
I've often heard people say,
hey, it feels kind of redundant,

00:10:52.794 --> 00:10:56.155
there's information that
I'm copying back and forth.

00:10:56.155 --> 00:11:00.318
And then I like to step back and
think these are two opportunities for

00:11:00.318 --> 00:11:02.450
you to share information with us.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:05.095
Take a look at those two materials.

00:11:05.095 --> 00:11:10.149
Do they necessarily have to say
the exact same details word for word?

00:11:10.149 --> 00:11:13.209
Yeah, things like your title or
the times that you worked and

00:11:13.209 --> 00:11:14.472
where might be the same.

00:11:14.472 --> 00:11:19.092
But there are different types of
information that you can convey using both

00:11:19.092 --> 00:11:19.686
pieces.

00:11:19.686 --> 00:11:24.246
So if you think about it that way,
use it as an opportunity so

00:11:24.246 --> 00:11:29.281
you know think through,
what do I want Stanford to know about me?

00:11:29.281 --> 00:11:31.816
Let's do this in a little bit more.

00:11:31.816 --> 00:11:35.854
Again, I'm not going to leave you
hanging so what should you include here?

00:11:35.854 --> 00:11:37.402
Again, up to you.

00:11:37.402 --> 00:11:38.506
By that, I mean,

00:11:38.506 --> 00:11:43.831
you're the only one who knows what are the
most meaningful things that you've done?

00:11:43.831 --> 00:11:46.102
What are the things that you
find the most important?

00:11:46.102 --> 00:11:47.347
What are you proud of?

00:11:47.347 --> 00:11:49.769
And then as you think about that,
like, why are you proud of it?

00:11:49.769 --> 00:11:52.517
How did you get to that place where
you're able to do those things?

00:11:52.517 --> 00:11:57.355
Some of those musings that can come
handy later on as people are curious

00:11:57.355 --> 00:11:59.407
to know more about your life.

00:11:59.407 --> 00:12:01.952
What are some of the things
that shaped your perspective?

00:12:01.952 --> 00:12:05.140
There is a question or
application about, hey,

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:08.031
what perspective do you
bring to the class?

00:12:08.031 --> 00:12:09.602
We'll talk a little bit more.

00:12:09.602 --> 00:12:13.172
I'm stumbling because I want to make
sure I get that phrasing right, because

00:12:13.172 --> 00:12:16.821
the wording is important to help you
understand what we're trying to get to.

00:12:16.821 --> 00:12:20.324
And then impact,
we talked a lot about impact, and

00:12:20.324 --> 00:12:24.738
that can mean anything like,
how is whatever space you're in.

00:12:24.738 --> 00:12:28.271
How's it different from your presence?

00:12:28.271 --> 00:12:31.509
What are things like scope,
scale, depth, context?

00:12:31.509 --> 00:12:36.992
What are some of those details that
might be handy for us to know about you?

00:12:36.992 --> 00:12:42.076
If in doubt, if it's important to you,
it's important to us.

00:12:42.076 --> 00:12:47.092
So, let's talk a little bit again,
step back that holistic reminder.

00:12:47.092 --> 00:12:51.811
We do not have a preference for where you
worked, what organizations you belong to,

00:12:51.811 --> 00:12:53.816
what school you might have gone to.

00:12:53.816 --> 00:12:58.724
So it's not about the name or
stature of that organization,

00:12:58.724 --> 00:13:03.357
but what are those footprints
that you have left behind?

00:13:03.357 --> 00:13:07.609
As you think about that and
connect that with intellectual vitality,

00:13:07.609 --> 00:13:12.523
demonstrated leadership potential,
personal qualities and contributions.

00:13:12.523 --> 00:13:17.347
All of those things, background agnostic,
context dependent, and

00:13:17.347 --> 00:13:18.910
what do I mean by that?

00:13:18.910 --> 00:13:22.099
Those three things can pop
up anywhere in your life.

00:13:22.099 --> 00:13:26.200
And one of the things that I love about
the Stanford evaluation process is will

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:29.497
recognize and celebrate that
wherever that might be for you.

00:13:29.497 --> 00:13:34.199
So maybe it comes with your church
involvement, your mosque, your synagogue.

00:13:34.199 --> 00:13:40.047
Maybe it's in a club or organization,
maybe it's something you do at home.

00:13:40.047 --> 00:13:44.905
There is a lot of leadership competencies
that you might be expressing in your

00:13:44.905 --> 00:13:46.487
home situation as well.

00:13:46.487 --> 00:13:51.319
So never underestimate with different
places that you might be making an impact.

00:13:51.319 --> 00:13:56.406
That context pieces that the way
that you're, some of these qualities

00:13:56.406 --> 00:14:01.256
might manifest is going to look
different depending on where you are.

00:14:01.256 --> 00:14:06.587
So leadership might look different if
you are a current college student versus

00:14:06.587 --> 00:14:11.688
if you are working and you have done
with your company for nine or ten years.

00:14:11.688 --> 00:14:15.732
Just because you're in different places
in your life doesn't mean one is more or

00:14:15.732 --> 00:14:16.567
less valuable.

00:14:16.567 --> 00:14:20.138
We just understand it looked different,
and that's okay.

00:14:20.138 --> 00:14:23.461
We want you to kind of
understand where you are,

00:14:23.461 --> 00:14:28.046
because that's where that starting
point for growth is, okay.

00:14:28.046 --> 00:14:34.684
Let's talk resume, I know we often
focus our attention on the essays,

00:14:34.684 --> 00:14:40.893
but for the GSB application it is
like a standard business resume.

00:14:40.893 --> 00:14:43.670
Try to keep it to one
page wherever possible.

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:46.542
That said,
there are some unique circumstances,

00:14:46.542 --> 00:14:50.704
extenuating circumstances where maybe
two pages might be appropriate.

00:14:50.704 --> 00:14:55.674
So the first one that people tend to
think about is if you've had ten or

00:14:55.674 --> 00:14:57.997
more years of work experience.

00:14:57.997 --> 00:15:02.184
Another one, sometimes you might
have had publications or a book or

00:15:02.184 --> 00:15:04.691
your written articles, a white paper.

00:15:04.691 --> 00:15:09.042
And those citations as you write them out,
like who were the authors,

00:15:09.042 --> 00:15:12.527
what year was it,
was that journal that takes up space.

00:15:12.527 --> 00:15:15.610
And so personally,
I think that's helpful information and

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:17.130
I know our staff does as well.

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:20.970
So sometimes there's instances where
it might be appropriate to go and

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:22.508
have into that second page.

00:15:22.508 --> 00:15:26.210
Again, we trust your judgement
there's room for flexibility here,

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:29.546
we're not going to dock you because
you go on to that second page.

00:15:29.546 --> 00:15:31.604
But really try your best of one page.

00:15:31.604 --> 00:15:36.054
Being succinct and direct and
clear in what you're trying to communicate

00:15:36.054 --> 00:15:40.660
is also a skill set that you're going to
need to leverage as a business leader.

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:45.868
So there's no better place to start
practicing that then your resume.

00:15:45.868 --> 00:15:50.624
Visual impression, so this is already
trending us into that style and

00:15:50.624 --> 00:15:52.085
how do I approach it.

00:15:52.085 --> 00:15:56.657
This kind of overlap in that Venn diagram,
but if you're thinking about content,

00:15:56.657 --> 00:15:58.830
how much writing should I put in there?

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:01.890
Step back, take a look.

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:06.480
Is it helpful or are you hoarding and
just adding all that detail?

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:11.240
Right, we are humans reading
on the side of the table.

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:15.571
So make sure that as you're writing
things are clear that you do read.

00:16:15.571 --> 00:16:19.567
You can definitely shrink margins if you
need to, but sometimes that tiny font or

00:16:19.567 --> 00:16:23.386
cramming in a lot of words can make it
really difficult to pull out the critical

00:16:23.386 --> 00:16:26.550
information that I should
be learning about you.

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:30.852
We do not have a resume format that we
require or ask you to do necessarily.

00:16:30.852 --> 00:16:34.524
Similar to the way you all have
different journeys through life,

00:16:34.524 --> 00:16:38.998
sometimes that does necessitate a little
bit of creative license and freedom to

00:16:38.998 --> 00:16:43.360
make sure that you're able to convey
it in a way that's appropriate for you.

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:48.450
Some industries might emphasize one thing,
others might emphasize another.

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:51.089
We want to make sure you're able to kind

00:16:51.089 --> 00:16:54.060
of speak your truth in
all of these spaces.

00:16:55.540 --> 00:16:58.290
So I have a little exercise here.

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:00.940
I actually really love doing resumes.

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:04.513
This is a thing that has
come about pretty recently.

00:17:04.513 --> 00:17:07.680
I think it's really fun.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:11.477
So we're going to play a game,
it's called who do you know more about?

00:17:11.477 --> 00:17:17.080
We have two individuals, all right,
individual A and individual B.

00:17:18.220 --> 00:17:24.980
A manages an aquatic conservation program,
that is that bullet point on their resume.

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:29.888
Person B in that same bullet point space,
created a public private

00:17:29.888 --> 00:17:34.550
action plan that studied seven
fish species from extinction.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:40.352
This might be a little obvious, and
I don't want to be too facetious with it,

00:17:40.352 --> 00:17:43.644
but I thought it would be kind
of fun to throw up a poll.

00:17:43.644 --> 00:17:45.740
So I'm going to put that out there.

00:17:45.740 --> 00:17:46.716
We're going to launch it.

00:17:46.716 --> 00:17:51.822
Who are you learning more about
from these one or two lines?

00:17:51.822 --> 00:17:54.230
Okay, it seems like it's
a pretty clear answer.

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:57.896
And there's a couple folks
that are putting in A,

00:17:57.896 --> 00:18:00.175
which is fine as well, [LAUGH].

00:18:00.175 --> 00:18:06.995
My point with this is there are ways for
you to describe the same job.

00:18:06.995 --> 00:18:11.946
When I made this example,I actually
have the same person in mind.

00:18:11.946 --> 00:18:17.164
And this is something that I often see in
resumes, so I'll go ahead and stop this.

00:18:17.164 --> 00:18:20.247
Thank you for entertaining that thought.

00:18:20.247 --> 00:18:26.069
But, When you're in your resume,
what's that bullet point doing?

00:18:26.069 --> 00:18:30.719
Is it describing what you
do versus what did you do?

00:18:30.719 --> 00:18:32.603
There's a slight nuance there.

00:18:32.603 --> 00:18:36.130
Sometimes when you're describing,
these are the things that I do.

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:40.180
It's more of a job description versus,
hey, here are the things that I have done.

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:41.940
What are some of those impacts?

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:44.714
That's when you're starting
to see if it's quantifiable,

00:18:44.714 --> 00:18:46.920
that's when the numbers come into play.

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:49.490
So really try to think about impact and

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:54.400
how you have left those organization
different or changed them.

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:57.988
And that might be one way for
you to think about, okay,

00:18:57.988 --> 00:19:03.440
how can I get maximum mileage out of
that limited real estate on my resume?

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:08.683
And again, each one of you has a,
Different format.

00:19:08.683 --> 00:19:11.922
Just ensure that it's clear,
easy to read, and

00:19:11.922 --> 00:19:16.517
that the things that you want us to
know about you can take those away.

00:19:16.517 --> 00:19:21.420
All right, now let's get into
the essays and the optional responses.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:26.257
I know there is a lot of questions, myths.

00:19:26.257 --> 00:19:28.480
This is where a lot of
myth busting happens.

00:19:28.480 --> 00:19:32.750
And so let's go here right,
let's get deep, let's get in the weeds.

00:19:32.750 --> 00:19:35.350
So, let's start with the essays.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:39.186
What matters most to you and why?

00:19:39.186 --> 00:19:41.580
And why Stanford?

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:45.390
Earlier I talked about letting
these questions be your guide.

00:19:45.390 --> 00:19:47.820
And notice, they are not descriptive.

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:52.790
They are not asking you for what matters
most to you in a business context.

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:56.420
It really is,
what matters most to you and why?

00:19:57.630 --> 00:20:01.240
I'd like to encourage all of
you to emphasize and why?

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:04.610
Because that is the question,
that's what we're asking.

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:07.956
There is a difference between
what matters most to you and

00:20:07.956 --> 00:20:11.840
here's evidence for it,
versus here's what matters to me.

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:14.155
And here's that introspection and

00:20:14.155 --> 00:20:18.640
reflection that helped me really
understand who are you because of?

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:24.011
We did an exercise in sharing your story
one where we listed different things and

00:20:24.011 --> 00:20:28.517
kind of talked to them as individuals
saying, because of you I am.

00:20:28.517 --> 00:20:34.980
And really helping you impact how those
instances might have impacted who you are.

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:38.030
And that's where that what matters
most to you and why comes from.

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:42.771
Dean Moss talks about a body of research
that says some of the most successful

00:20:42.771 --> 00:20:47.734
leaders out there are the ones who lead
from a place of mission, and values, and

00:20:47.734 --> 00:20:49.150
purpose.

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:50.670
What are yours?

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:54.595
This internal dialogue that you
are engaging with in this question is also

00:20:54.595 --> 00:20:58.773
going to be reflective of some of that
internal dialogue, that world of feedback

00:20:58.773 --> 00:21:02.984
that you're going to be engaging with at
the GSB in that leadership curriculum.

00:21:02.984 --> 00:21:05.250
Why should someone follow you?

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:09.333
Draw that attention inward, and hopefully
this question is doing that as well.

00:21:09.333 --> 00:21:11.573
Why Stanford?

00:21:11.573 --> 00:21:14.150
Two words, so
many places you can go with them.

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:19.175
Maybe the subtext here is why Stanford for
you?

00:21:19.175 --> 00:21:22.930
Center yourself in all of the responses.

00:21:22.930 --> 00:21:25.670
Some folks might,
the mission might resonate for them.

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:28.358
For others,
it's because their ability to explore and

00:21:28.358 --> 00:21:32.270
pivot into another place is going to
be facilitated by the stuff here.

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:34.906
We have no expectations.

00:21:34.906 --> 00:21:39.720
We don't think one rationale is more
important or compelling than another.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:45.280
What I want to learn about is what are
your motivations for coming here, right?

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:47.070
And so that's where that comes from.

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:50.084
I want to learn about you.

00:21:50.084 --> 00:21:53.477
Okay, there are the optional
responses as well.

00:21:53.477 --> 00:21:55.579
And so where these came from,

00:21:55.579 --> 00:22:00.207
I think helps illustrate how we're
thinking about the essays and

00:22:00.207 --> 00:22:05.775
these responses as a way to learn about
you kind of in a really interesting way.

00:22:05.775 --> 00:22:10.292
So what we found in the past is that
what matters most to you and why essay,

00:22:10.292 --> 00:22:13.865
people were putting in their
accomplishments in there.

00:22:13.865 --> 00:22:15.941
Which definitely things to be proud of,

00:22:15.941 --> 00:22:20.630
but they didn't always necessarily connect
with the things that mattered to them.

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:26.190
So it almost felt like they were
shoving a round peg into a square hole.

00:22:26.190 --> 00:22:30.130
So that's where these optional responses
came in, especially that impact one.

00:22:30.130 --> 00:22:33.750
We want to create a separate place
because we do want to know about that.

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:36.330
We do want to know about
the impact you've created.

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:42.356
But we also want you to be able to answer
the questions kind of with honesty and

00:22:42.356 --> 00:22:45.933
truth, right and
not have to try to squeeze or

00:22:45.933 --> 00:22:50.097
kind of mix things up just
to get all of these bits in.

00:22:50.097 --> 00:22:51.790
So what are these questions?

00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:55.240
There we go.

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:59.267
Think about times you've created a
positive impact, whether in professional,

00:22:59.267 --> 00:23:02.350
extracurricular, academic,
or other settings.

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:03.910
What was your impact?

00:23:03.910 --> 00:23:07.690
What made it significant to you or others?

00:23:07.690 --> 00:23:10.970
Pretty clear cut here,
we're asking you directly.

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:16.540
And one of the things here is that you
don't necessarily have to use flowy prose.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:20.756
Take how much space you have and
how much runway as a clue to, hey,

00:23:20.756 --> 00:23:23.835
maybe this is where I can be more direct.

00:23:23.835 --> 00:23:27.389
Maybe I'm just giving the facts so
that they know kind of right away,

00:23:27.389 --> 00:23:28.515
what's going on.

00:23:28.515 --> 00:23:31.515
What's the context, scope, scale?

00:23:31.515 --> 00:23:33.775
Why is this meaningful and significant?

00:23:34.835 --> 00:23:39.001
The second one, tell us about a time
within the last three years when your

00:23:39.001 --> 00:23:44.990
background influenced your participation
in a situation, interaction, or project.

00:23:44.990 --> 00:23:48.844
Again, very clear about kind of
the context and circumstances that were

00:23:48.844 --> 00:23:52.940
asking you, but also very open
ended when we say background.

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:56.150
No, that could be anything
about who you are.

00:23:56.150 --> 00:24:00.008
Again, in session one of starting
your story, we practiced and

00:24:00.008 --> 00:24:02.866
went through a couple different exercises,
so

00:24:02.866 --> 00:24:06.521
that you might be able to think
through okay, hey, who am I?

00:24:06.521 --> 00:24:10.710
And what does that mean as I think about
both business school and life beyond?

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:14.662
And that could be one place to go if
you'd like a little bit of inspiration,

00:24:14.662 --> 00:24:17.260
if you want to go through
this optional question.

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:21.260
Okay, I'm going to call this
section quick questions.

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.032
So this is actually drawn
from things this week.

00:24:24.032 --> 00:24:27.224
And we're going to speed through this and
hopefully it'll cover some

00:24:27.224 --> 00:24:29.810
of the questions that you
have in the Q&amp;A box, right?

00:24:29.810 --> 00:24:31.590
So do my essays have to connect?

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:33.560
What if I talk about the same things?

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:34.670
Will it be redundant?

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:39.800
We're going to go through these one by
one and we're going to hammer them out.

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:41.670
Do my essays have to connect?

00:24:41.670 --> 00:24:42.970
The answer is no.

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:45.796
Sometimes the things that
matter the most to you,

00:24:45.796 --> 00:24:50.090
aren't the reasons that you want to
go to Stanford, and that's fine.

00:24:50.090 --> 00:24:54.060
We want you to use those spaces in a way
that makes the most sense for you.

00:24:55.850 --> 00:24:59.160
What if I talk about the same things,
will it be redundant?

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:00.821
So you can talk about the same thing,

00:25:00.821 --> 00:25:03.960
